Abstract
This work presents a complete first-principles framework grounded in a combinatorial network. All physical phenomena — space, time, matter, gravitation, electromagnetism, nuclear forces, superconductivity, and celestial evolution — are reduced to three primitive objects: nodes, directed edges, and shared links. Matter consists of topological knots woven from shared links. Light is a coupled transverse wave of shared link density and orientation. Gravitation is the large-number statistical effect of total shared link count. The superconducting state is strictly defined by the global phase-locking condition DijΘ=0DijΘ=0. Nuclear transitions proceed through two channels: defect-complementary weaving (Path I) and forced chain reconnection (Path II). The geometric origin of the Pauli exclusion principle, the geometric decoupling of isotopic fission thresholds, the discrete geometric formulation of the London equations and the Meissner effect, and the topological necessity of flux quantization are derived. A complete celestial evolutionary spectrum from solid planets to quasars is constructed. Twenty testable predictions are presented, and twenty-one open problems are explicitly listed. General relativity and quantum field theory are repositioned within TDMU as the macroscopic continuum limit and the low-energy weak-field approximation of the membrane, respectively. The standard-model "singularity" is replaced by the connection-saturated state — d(i)=dmax⁡(i),∀id(i)=dmax(i),∀i — a finite graph-theoretic extremum, fully defined and computable. The framework contains no dark matter or dark energy as independent concepts. All physical laws are emergent manifestations of combinatorial network geometry.
Keywords: Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe; combinatorial network; recursive weaving; nuclear transition channels; geometric origin of superconductivity; celestial evolution; connection-saturated state; light-gravity decoupling
