On the Essential Distinction Between "Singularity" and "Connection-Saturated State":
The "singularity" in standard models is a mathematical divergence point — quantities approach infinity, the theory fails at that point, and physical quantities cannot be defined. No such object exists in TDMU.
The "connection-saturated state" in TDMU is a finite graph-theoretic extremum: when every node in the network attains its maximum allowable degree, the network is connection-saturated. This state is precisely described by the formula d(i)=dmax⁡(i),∀id(i)=dmax(i),∀i, corresponding to the complete graph KNKN or the limit of a maximally connected graph. It is finite, determinate, and entirely within the descriptive scope of the theory.
The standard-model "singularity" is where the theory cannot reach. TDMU's "connection-saturated state" is the maximally connected state that the theory can precisely describe and compute. There is no analogical relation between them.
________________________________________
Special Declaration on "Dark Matter" and "Dark Energy":
This framework does not contain "dark matter" or "dark energy" as independent concepts. All observational effects attributed by standard models to dark matter — galactic rotation curves, gravitational lensing, structure formation — are in TDMU the sum of shared-link convergence contributions from known topological knot aggregates (compact topological stars, woven solid remnants, MACHOs, planetary remnants, etc.). All observational effects attributed by standard models to dark energy — accelerated membrane expansion — are in TDMU the geometric hydrostatic pressure exerted on the membrane wall by external substrate circulation (Axiom 12). These two classes of phenomena require no additional degrees of freedom or species in TDMU. The terms "dark matter" and "dark energy" from standard models are not recognized in this framework and do not correspond to any TDMU concepts.
