Part I: Ontology
Axiom 1: The Substrate
There exists a discrete, background-free combinatorial network MM, constituted by nodes and directed edges connecting them.
This network is embedded in no space. It evolves in no pre-existing time. The sole primitive relation between nodes and edges is "connected" or "not connected." The sole primitive attribute of an edge is its direction — from one node to another.
The sole dynamics of the substrate is local reconnection: when two edges meet at a node, they may disconnect and reconnect according to directional rules. Reconnection is local — it involves only the few edges sharing a given node, never distant structures.
Directional Rule: For any reconnection operation, along any closed path traversal, the difference between the number of counterclockwise edges and clockwise edges remains invariant.
The substrate contains no physical quantities. No mass, no energy, no charge, no field. Only nodes, directed edges, and the connection relations between them.
Definition 1.1: Loop
A closed path beginning from a node, proceeding along directed edges, and returning to the starting node, is called a loop. Loops are the only structures in the substrate possessing "global recognizability."
Definition 1.2: Topological Charge
Along any closed loop, the net directional sum — the count of counterclockwise traversals minus clockwise traversals — is an integer. This integer is conserved under reconnection operations, as guaranteed by the directional rule.
This integer is called the loop's topological charge.
The topological charge is not a pre-assumed physical quantity. It is the naming of a purely geometric fact: "the directional sum along a closed path."
Axiom 2: Spontaneous Membrane Emergence
When, within some closed region of the substrate, loop density (the number of closed paths per unit node count) exceeds a critical value, the loops within that region tend through collective reconnection to weave a closed surface. This surface partitions the region into "interior" and "exterior" — interior loop density differs from exterior, and the surface itself becomes the boundary between the two regions.
This closed surface is called the membrane.
The membrane is not "placed into" the substrate. It is a structural pattern toward which the substrate naturally tends under specific loop density conditions. The membrane is a state of the substrate.
Honest Annotation: The exact numerical value of the critical density cannot currently be derived analytically from pure combinatorial rules. The model qualitatively describes it as "the value above which the loop connectivity of a closed region suffices to support a closed surface." Determination of this value awaits future numerical simulation or analytic proof of combinatorial dynamics.
Axiom 3: Weaving Hierarchies
Material structures are constituted by topological knots (woven subgraphs that do not spontaneously disassemble) arranged in recursive hierarchies. Interactions between topological knots are mediated through shared links. Every node on this recursive growth tree is a topological knot of some specific order, whose primordial brick count is determined by the brick counts of its predecessor knots.
Corollary 3.1: Hierarchical Cutoff Criterion
The shared link network between topological knots is counted as effectively interacting only when the knot separation is less than or equal to a finite integer multiple of the smaller knot's recursive growth radius.
Specifically, let topological knot AA have recursive order mm, determined by its primordial brick count NANA (with NA=4mNA=4m under a specific packing configuration). Its intrinsic combinatorial radius is rA=⌈NA1/3⌉rA=⌈NA1/3⌉ (in node steps).
When the center-to-center distance dAB>κ⋅(rA+rB)dAB>κ⋅(rA+rB), with κκ a screening factor determined by local loop density, κ∈[1,3]κ∈[1,3], the total shared link count NlinksNlinks decays exponentially below the threshold NthreshNthresh, effectively vanishing in macroscopic physics.
The origin of this cutoff: intermediate loop density in the substrate, beyond distances rA+rBrA+rB, cannot sustain stable weaving channels — any additional links spontaneously reconnect and dissolve because intermediate nodes lack sufficient topological charge support.
Physical Consequences:
•	At nuclear scales (phenomenologically ∼10−15∼10−15 m): the cutoff radius far exceeds nucleon spacing; shared link count is geometrically locked at maximum saturation — phenomenologically manifested as the short-range saturation of the strong nuclear force.
•	At atomic scales (phenomenologically ∼10−10∼10−10 m): the cutoff radius precisely matches electron cloud extent; shared link count declines naturally with distance according to combinatorial path counts — phenomenologically manifested as the 1/r1/r form of the Coulomb potential.
•	At cosmic scales (phenomenologically ≳1022≳1022 m): the cutoff radius matches the highest order of the recursive growth tree (galaxy cluster scale); beyond this scale shared link counts vanish — phenomenologically manifested as gravitational screening and decoupling from membrane expansion.
