Part II: Emergence of Spacetime
Axiom 4: Combinatorial Distance
The distance between two nodes is defined as the number of edges along the shortest path connecting them. The distance between two structures is defined as the number of edges along the shortest path connecting their respective regions.
Distance is not a pre-existing metric in space. It is a direct reading of connection relations between nodes.
Axiom 5: Space
The totality of distance relations among all structures upon the membrane constitutes the membrane's spatial structure.
Space is not a container holding the membrane. It is the collection of relations among structures upon it.
When a structure moves, it does not travel through space — it alters its connection relations with all other structures. "Motion" is the record of reconnection history in distance relations.
Axiom 6: Time
The temporal order between two events AA and BB on the membrane is determined by the cumulative reconnection event count along paths connecting them. If for any path from AA to BB the total reconnection count is NN, with N>0N>0, then AA precedes BB; if N=0N=0, then AA and BB are simultaneous.
Time is not a dimension, not a coordinate, not a parameter. It is cumulative counting of reconnection events.
Honest Annotation on the Arrow of Time:
Reconnection processes are logically reversible. However, reversal requires the system to retain complete connection-state information prior to reconnection. In a combinatorial network, the recoverability of this information decays exponentially with reconnection history length. The arrow of time is thus a statistical fact, not a logical necessity.
The model assumes that our particular membrane region exists in a state of "nearly irreversible" reconnection. The rigorous foundation of this assumption awaits proof.
