Part II-B: Foundational Theorem — The Status of Relativity and Quantum Physics within TDMU
Theorem 0: The Geometric Origin of the Standard Model Conflict
In standard theory, general relativity and quantum field theory share an unexamined presupposition: a pre-existing continuous spacetime manifold. Their conflict stems from the attempt to house both within this single mathematical container:
•	General Relativity: defines the metric tensor gμνgμν on the continuous manifold; gravitation is manifold curvature.
•	Quantum Field Theory: defines fiber bundles on the same manifold; the three forces are gauge fields (U(1),SU(2),SU(3)U(1),SU(2),SU(3)) on these bundles.
This conflict is logically inevitable under the continuous manifold presupposition — unification is in principle impossible so long as standard theory refuses to examine the presupposition of "continuous spacetime as a pre-existing background."
Within TDMU, this presupposition is completely eliminated.
________________________________________
