Theorem 3: Resolution of the Standard Model Conflict within TDMU
The fundamental conflicts within standard theory — continuous spacetime vs quantum fluctuations, determinism vs probability, locality vs non-local entanglement — have no existence in the TDMU substrate, because both theories are two projections of the same discrete combinatorial network at different scale cutoffs.
Conflict Source (Standard Theory)	TDMU Resolution Mechanism
Continuous spacetime vs discrete quantum	Spacetime is itself the emergent statistical effect of a discrete network (Axioms 4, 5, 6); no "pre-existing continuous background" exists
Determinism (GR field equations) vs probability (QFT scattering amplitudes)	Both are manifestations of the same reconnection rules under different statistical limits — deterministic limit corresponds to Nlinks→∞Nlinks→∞, probabilistic limit to Nlinks≪∞Nlinks≪∞

Locality (GR light cone) vs non-locality (quantum entanglement)	Light cone is characteristic surface of transverse wave propagation; entanglement is non-spatial connection via shared internal sutures (Part VI); no contradiction arises because "space" plays no fundamental role in entanglement
Quantum gravity problem (quantizing the metric)	Gravitation is statistical counting of NtotalNtotal, not a field — it cannot be quantized, and need not be quantized
Core Conclusion:
The "quantum gravity" problem of standard theory is a pseudo-problem — it originates from the mistaken identification of a statistical quantity (gravitation: link counting) with a dynamical quantity (metric field), followed by an attempt to describe it in the language of quantum mechanics.
Within TDMU, gravitation need not be quantized. It requires only redefinition as the statistical effect of total shared link count.
