Corollary: Final Adjudication of the Standard Model Unification Program
Within the TDMU framework, the "unification" of general relativity and quantum field theory is a task in principle impossible within the standard theory paradigm — because it attempts to force two approximate descriptions from different scales and different statistical limits into a single continuous spacetime manifold mathematical framework.
Only when the presupposition of continuous spacetime is completely eliminated and replaced by the discrete geometry of a combinatorial network can gravitational and quantum phenomena be described by a single set of first principles — nodes, directed edges, shared links, recursive weaving, hierarchical cutoff.
This unification is not symmetry unification (fitting all forces into a single large group), but geometric homology unification — all forces redefined as five different readings of the same network.
