Part III: Emergence of Material Structure — Recursive Weaving and Energy Scale Relativity
Axiom 7: Topological Knot
A woven subgraph on the membrane, composed of loops, that does not spontaneously disassemble under reconnection dynamics, is called a topological knot.
A subgraph is non-disassembling if and only if: the number of successive reconnections required to separate it from the rest of the membrane exceeds a threshold. This threshold prevents any local reconnection operation from releasing internal stress without destroying the overall structure.
Matter does not consist of pre-existing particles. It is a topological knot on the membrane that has attained stability conditions.
Definition 7.1: Stability Parameters of Topological Knots
The stability of a topological knot is determined by two factors:
1.	Total interior link count: the number of independent connection paths among interior loops. Higher total counts require more reconnection operations for disassembly.
2.	Boundary connectivity: the number of connection paths between the knot surface and the exterior. Lower boundary connectivity impedes external reconnection intrusion into the interior.
Definition 7.2: Topological Defects and Incomplete Surface Weaving
No topological knot surface can achieve absolutely perfect closure — there invariably exist regions not fully covered by existing weave, called topological defects. Defect size and distribution depend on knot weave complexity: knots with fewer primordial bricks have looser weaves with larger defect area fractions; knots with more primordial bricks have tighter weaves with smaller defect area fractions.
Topological defects are the "active sites" through which topological knots interact. Whether two topological knots can undergo defect-complementary weaving depends entirely on the geometric compatibility of their surface defects.
Definition 7.3: Primordial Brick
The smallest topological knot on the membrane capable of independent existence and of establishing stable shared links with other topological knots is called a primordial brick.
The primordial brick is the effective fundamental unit at the current energy scale. Its "fundamentality" is energy-scale dependent — if future investigation at higher energy scales reveals that the primordial brick is composed of smaller structures, the model naturally extends.
Definition 7.4: Inertia of the Primordial Brick
The capacity of a primordial brick to resist directional reconnection — that is, the number of reconnection events required to alter its loop's net directional sum — defines its inertia. Inertia is the dynamical manifestation of topological charge. This property is phenomenologically designated "mass."
Axiom 8: Recursive Growth and Topological Knot Spectra
Topological knots do not exist in isolation. Under appropriate energy scales and collision conditions, smaller stable topological knots may combine into larger stable topological knots through defect-complementary weaving. Since weaving incompleteness is universal, every topological knot surface possesses defects available for matching. When the defects of two smaller knots are geometrically complementary, they interlock to form a larger weave structure — this process is governed entirely by the geometric properties of both parties' defects.
This combination is not arbitrary. The defect distributions of smaller knots capable of stable combination must satisfy specific topological compatibility conditions: the defect distribution of the new knot must be naturally inheritable through reconnection from the two predecessor knots' defect distributions, and the combined weave network must attain the new stability threshold. This compatibility condition does not impose a unique numerical sequence constraint on the predecessor primordial brick counts, but tends to select combinations whose defect distributions "seamlessly fit." This naturally generates a recursive growth tree of topological knots — from primordial bricks, generation by generation, forming increasingly complex and massive stable knots.
Definition 8.1: Electron Brick
On the recursive growth tree, a stable topological knot composed of four primordial bricks in a specific weave configuration is called an electron brick. It is the smallest independent stable knot at the current cosmic energy scale, the fundamental building block of all more complex material structures.
The interior structure of the electron brick likewise follows recursive weaving principles: it possesses a solid core formed by tight weaving of those four primordial bricks, and a possible soft outer layer composed of deeper primordial bricks — earlier generations on the recursive tree — attached to the core through defects. At the current low cosmic energy scale, the mass contribution of this outer layer is extremely small and may be neglected.
Definition 8.2: Exchange Reconnection Count and the Geometric Compulsion of Pauli Exclusion
Definition 8.2a (Exchange Reconnection Count SS): Let pA,pBpA,pB be one dangling link port each on the surface of two identical topological knots (electron bricks). The minimum number of local reconnection operations required to move pApA to pBpB's original position and pBpB to pApA's original position is the exchange reconnection count SS.
Lemma 8.1 (Exchange Parity): For two identical topological knots occupying the same order on the recursive growth tree, the exchange reconnection count SS of their ports is necessarily odd.
Proof Outline: Exchange of two identical topological knots must be accomplished through braid exchange (circulation along closed loops, not direct node crossing). Each local reconnection operation changes the target node of one directed edge. For a complete braid exchange of two identical objects, the generator sequence length kk of the braid group BNBN must satisfy k≡1(mod2)k≡1(mod2). A minimal constructive example requires 3 local reconnections. Therefore, SS is necessarily odd.
Theorem 8.1 (Geometric Compulsion of Pauli Exclusion): Two identical electron bricks cannot occupy completely identical geometric positions and phase states.
Proof: If two identical electron bricks occupied completely identical geometric states, the exchange operation would reduce to the identity operation — 0 reconnection steps, even. Lemma 8.1 requires the exchange reconnection count to be odd. The contradiction odd = even forces the assumption to be false. Two identical electron bricks must maintain at least one geometric degree of freedom difference — either phase difference Δθ≥1Δθ≥1, or occupying different spatial winding modes.
Definition 8.3: Baryon Bricks (Phenomenologically Corresponding to Protons and Neutrons) and Binary Layered Structure
At orders far above the electron brick on the recursive growth tree, there exist massive stable topological knots composed of large numbers of electron bricks and deeper primordial bricks woven according to recursive rules.
The structure of this class of topological knots exhibits a universal binary layered pattern:
•	Solid Core: composed of large numbers of electron bricks or equivalent primordial bricks in closest packing arrangement. For topological knots reaching macroscopic scales, cubic close packing is the optimal arrangement; in this limit, the core primordial brick count approaches integer multiples of 123=1728123=1728. The core provides high binding energy resisting disassembly.
•	Soft Outer Layer: composed of large numbers of primordial bricks or small topological knots floating on the core surface, attached to the core through defects, forming a deformable buffer layer. Outer layer primordial bricks may slide under external force, absorbing collision energy. The defect distribution of the outer layer determines the macroscopic charge of the topological knot — positive, negative, or neutral — depending on the net directional sum of outer layer defects.
These two stable configurations phenomenologically correspond respectively to the proton (positive charge) and neutron (neutral charge). Both share the "solid core + soft outer layer" structural pattern, differing only in the net topological charge attribute of the outer layer defects.
This "solid interior, soft exterior" binary structure is a universal design of recursive weaving, applicable across all levels from electron bricks to baryon bricks to higher-order atomic nuclei.
Definition 8.4: Total Compressive Binding Energy (EcompEcomp)
As a closed weave network, a topological knot possesses a structural threshold resisting external compression. This threshold, determined jointly by all interior link strengths, weave configuration, and defect distribution, is the total compressive binding energy, denoted EcompEcomp.
EcompEcomp is the single unified quantity of a topological knot's resistance to compression, presupposing no interior microscopic decomposition structure. Its essence is the structural resistive force of the topological knot as a closed weave network maintaining its configuration. This resistive force exists in all topological knots regardless of electrical charge.
Definition 8.4a (Hierarchical Compressive Tensor): The compressive binding energy EcompEcomp of an atomic nucleus decomposes into three geometrically independent contributions:
Ecomp=Vcore⋅Score+Vshell⋅Sshell+I(suture)Ecomp=Vcore⋅Score+Vshell⋅Sshell+I(suture)
Where:
•	VcoreVcore: core close-packed primordial brick unit count, determining the primary mass number of the nucleus.
•	ScoreScore: core average link saturation. For perfect close packing (such as 123123 hypercube closure), Score→1Score→1.
•	VshellVshell: effective primordial brick unit count in the outer shell participating in chemical bonding.
•	SshellSshell: shell port saturation, determining chemical valence and reactivity.
•	I(suture)I(suture): topological stress concentration coefficient at the core-shell transition suture, determined by deviation of suture layer primordial brick arrangement from the ideal closure configuration.
Theorem 8.2: Chemical-Nuclear Decoupling Theorem
The spatial orientation of shell dangling ports (i.e., chemical valence) depends solely on SshellSshell and the arrangement of shell ports, independent of the total core primordial brick count VcoreVcore. The suture stress concentration coefficient II is highly sensitive to discrete variations in core primordial brick count.
Corollary 8.1 (Vacancy Stress Concentration): If the core primordial brick arrangement contains vacancies — actual primordial brick count below the ideal closure count for that weave-tree order — then the suture topological stress concentration coefficient I>0I>0. With no vacancies, I≈0I≈0.
Corollary 8.2 (Fission Threshold Difference in Uranium Isotopes):
•	238U238U: core primordial brick arrangement matches the complete 123123 hypercube closure, I238≈0I238≈0, fission trigger threshold high.
•	235U235U: core lacks 3 primordial brick units; vacancies yield I235>0I235>0, fission trigger threshold significantly lower than 238U238U.
Their chemical properties (SshellSshell) are identical — shell port arrangement is unaffected by core vacancies.
Honest Annotation: The precise numerical value of II depends on vacancy positions within the closure, vacancy count, and suture geometry. Complete calculation requires systematic analysis of stress distributions for 123123 hypercubes under various vacancy configurations. This work provides a qualitatively strict criterion (I>0I>0 if and only if vacancies are present), not numerical values.
