Part V: Elemental Abundance and the Iron Peak — Three-Factor Criterion
5.1 The Three-Factor Criterion
Within the reaction network constituted by the four channels, the cosmic abundance of any nuclide is jointly determined by three probabilities:
1.	Synthesis Probability: the probability that the nuclide is produced through various precursor channels. If a channel is blocked by Path III, its contribution is zero, though the nuclide may still be synthesized through other channels.
2.	Continued Aggregation Probability: the probability that the nuclide, as a reactant, continues aggregating into heavier nuclides through Path I or Path II.
3.	Fission Probability: the probability that the nuclide undergoes stress tearing back into lighter nuclei.
These three jointly determine the net retention on the mass spectrum:
Net Retention∝Synthesis Probability×(1−Continued Aggregation Probability−Fission Probability)Net Retention∝Synthesis Probability×(1−Continued Aggregation Probability−Fission Probability)
5.2 Jump Pathways and Sequential Pathways
Within this framework, there exists no "mandatory path" across the nuclide mass spectrum. Any two topological knots — regardless of light, medium, or heavy — may aggregate provided their defects match. Reaction probability depends on the geometric compatibility of both parties' defects, with no rigid constraint from their positions on the mass spectrum.
Jump pathways and sequential pathways are not mutually exclusive but two parallel channels whose weights are dynamically determined by precursor abundance ratios. In the early universe, with uniform material abundance, jump pathways become the dominant channel, enabling exponential acceleration of structure growth — nuclides of arbitrary mass number may aggregate directly without sequential accumulation.
5.3 The Iron Peak: A Specific Manifestation of the Three-Factor Criterion
The prominence of iron-56 abundance is a specific manifestation of the above three-factor criterion at mass number 56:
•	Synthesis Probability: The nuclear flow can reach mass number 56 through both sequential and jump pathways.
•	Continued Aggregation Probability: The defect area fraction of mass-number-56 topological knots is extremely small; their probability as reactants to continue aggregating upward is low.
•	Fission Probability: The interior suture stress distribution of mass-number-56 topological knots is balanced; their probability of being torn back to lighter nuclei is also low.
Superposition of the three: the nuclear flow can reach mass number 56, but once there, both forward and backward rates are low — net accumulation. This is the complete origin of the iron peak.
5.4 The Astrophysical Role of Iron Nuclei
Abundance Level: The iron peak's position is determined by the three-factor criterion.
Structural Level: The special role of iron nuclei in stellar evolution originates not from their abundance peak status, but from their extremely high total compressive binding energy EcompEcomp. The Path-I aggregation probability of iron nuclei is extremely low, but EcompEcomp is extremely high, allowing iron cores to withstand immense gravitational compressive energy without triggering Path II. When surrounding material continues accumulating until gravitational compressive energy exceeds the iron core's EcompEcomp threshold, Path II is triggered. This threshold corresponds phenomenologically to ∼1.4M⊙∼1.4M⊙, explaining the characteristic features of iron-core collapse events at this mass.
The prominence of the iron peak in abundance and the structural rigidity of iron nuclei are two distinct problems, determined respectively by the three-factor criterion and EcompEcomp, and should not be conflated.
