Part VI: Interactions — Geometry of Shared Links and Light-Gravity Decoupling
Axiom 9: Shared Links
Between two topological knots, multiple connection paths established through intermediate loops on the membrane are called shared links. Shared links are the sole interaction mediators between topological knots.
There exist no independent "force" entities. All interactions are manifestations of the geometric properties of shared links.
Definition 9.1: The Five Geometric Parameters of Shared Links
All interactions reduce to the following five geometric parameters of shared links:
Parameter	Definition	Phenomenological Manifestation
Total link count	Number of independent connection paths between two topological knots	Gravitational strength
Link orientation	Statistical uniformity of shared link distribution across spatial directions	Magnetic strength
Link density gradient	Rate of change of link count across space	Electric strength
Link saturation	Ratio of existing links to all possible connection paths	Strong nuclear force (attraction/repulsion)
Link fragility	Probability of disconnection under perturbation	Weak force decay rate
6.1 Geometric Freedom Distinction Between Light and Gravity
Three independent geometric freedoms of the shared link network must be strictly distinguished:
Freedom 1: Total Link Count NtotalNtotal — Static/Quasi-static Scalar Counting
•	Determines gravitation and torque. This total is unaffected by density gradient potential wells.
•	Even when radial propagating waves are fully confined, the corresponding retractive stress from this total propagates across vast radii to exert influence on external material.
•	Physical Correspondence: Gravitational work, "shaftless drive" rigid torque transmission.
Freedom 2: Density Gradient ∇ρlink∇ρlink and Orientation Wave δAδA — Dynamic Transverse Wave Perturbations
•	Determines light — phenomenologically electromagnetic radiation. First- and second-order gradient potential wells act only on these transverse waves.
•	Does not act on static tension counting.
•	Physical Correspondence: Normal light radiation, photon capture.
Freedom 3 — Independent Channel: Density Fluctuation Longitudinal Wave δρlinkδρlink
•	Longitudinal compression-extension stress waves of shared link density — phenomenological phonon modes.
•	The optical potential well acts only on transverse waves δAδA; longitudinal waves are not constrained by this potential well.
•	Physical Correspondence: Background gradient longitudinal wave radiation — non-normal light leakage.
Core Theorem (Light-Gravity Decoupling Theorem):
Gravitation (NtotalNtotal) and light (δAδA) are carried by completely independent geometric freedoms in TDMU. Light capture does not imply gravitational isolation; gravitational penetration of the capture surface does not imply light escape. This decoupling constitutes the fundamental divergence between TDMU and general relativity.
6.2 Construction of Discrete Gauge Structure
Definition 9.2 (Combinatorial Covariant Derivative): Let the discrete phase field of shared links be Θ(ri)Θ(ri), defined on lattice node ii. Let AijAij be the orientation tensor contribution of background shared links between nodes i,ji,j. Define the combinatorial covariant derivative:
DijΘ≡Θ(rj)−Θ(ri)−AijDijΘ≡Θ(rj)−Θ(ri)−Aij
Where:
•	Θ(ri)∈ZΘ(ri)∈Z is the phase at node ii — reconnection step count
•	AijAij is the background orientation field between nodes i,ji,j due to periodic nuclear arrangement, defined as the minimum reconnection steps along i→ji→j
•	All quantities are purely discrete, independent of continuous spatial metrics
6.3 Geometric Definition of the Superconducting State
Definition 9.3 (Superconducting State): If and only if for all nearest-neighbor node pairs ⟨i,j⟩⟨i,j⟩, the covariant derivative vanishes:
DijΘ=0,∀⟨i,j⟩∈conducting networkDijΘ=0,∀⟨i,j⟩∈conducting network
The system is in global phase locking — all shared link helical motions are perfectly synchronized.
6.4 Discrete Derivation of the London First Equation
Phenomenological current density JJ in TDMU is defined as directional shared link flux. When the system deviates from perfect superconducting state but maintains phase coherence, the covariant derivative possesses residual values. This residual drives directional link flux:
J∝∑⟨i,j⟩DijΘJ∝⟨i,j⟩∑DijΘ
In the continuum limit, DijΘDijΘ corresponds to the covariant derivative ∇Θ−A∇Θ−A. Under the London gauge — ∇⋅A=0∇⋅A=0, superconducting phase rigidity — the phase gradient term may be absorbed as gauge redundancy or set to zero, yielding:
J=−1ΛAJ=−Λ1A
where ΛΛ is the shared link network's geometric inertial constant, determined by the total link count per unit volume NtotalNtotal: Λ∝1/NtotalΛ∝1/Ntotal. This is the purely discrete form of the London first equation.
6.5 Geometric Necessity of the Meissner Effect
Taking the discrete curl of the London equation — summation along the smallest closed path — yields:
∇×J=−1Λ(∇×A)=−1ΛB∇×J=−Λ1(∇×A)=−Λ1B
Combining with the magnetostatic form of Ampère's law ∇×B=μ0J∇×B=μ0J, taking the curl again and utilizing the vector identity ∇×(∇×B)=−∇2B∇×(∇×B)=−∇2B under the Coulomb gauge ∇⋅B=0∇⋅B=0, yields:
∇2B=μ0ΛB∇2B=Λμ0B
With penetration depth λL≡Λ/μ0λL≡Λ/μ0, the solution is:
B(r)=B(0) e−r/λLB(r)=B(0)e−r/λL
This proves exponential decay of magnetic field within a superconductor — the geometric origin of the Meissner effect.
6.6 Topological Necessity of Flux Quantization
Consider a closed path CC around a superconducting ring. The phase ΘΘ must return to the same discrete count after one full circuit; the total phase change is therefore an integer multiple of 2π2π — ΘΘ is a modulo-2π2π helical step count.
Summing the covariant derivative along the closed path:
∮CDΘ⋅dl=2πn,n∈Z∮CDΘ⋅dl=2πn,n∈Z
In the superconducting state, DΘ=0DΘ=0 holds within the ring body, but along the closed path enclosing the ring hole, the contribution of AA is not canceled. Therefore:
∮CDΘ⋅dl=−∮CA⋅dl=−Φ∮CDΘ⋅dl=−∮CA⋅dl=−Φ
Combining yields Φ=−2πnΦ=−2πn. Magnetic flux in TDMU geometric natural units is strictly quantized as integer multiples of the fundamental unit Φ0=2πΦ0=2π.
6.7 Geometric Definitions of the Five Fundamental Interactions
Definition 9.4: Gravitation
The greater the total shared link count between two topological knots, the stronger the membrane's tendency to reduce overall stress — this tendency phenomenologically manifests as pulling the two knots toward each other.
Gravitation is the large-number statistical effect of total shared link count. No graviton is required.
Definition 9.5: Magnetism
When shared links exhibit statistically non-uniform distribution across spatial directions, a macroscopic torsional stress field arises. The streamlines of this stress field are magnetic field lines.
The closure of magnetic field lines is topologically necessary — shared links cannot possess isolated endpoints in space. Magnetic monopoles are topologically forbidden.
Definition 9.6: Electricity
The density gradient of shared links generates normal stress waves. Stress differences exist between regions of density excess and density deficit:
•	Excess-excess or deficit-deficit: repulsive stress difference
•	Excess-deficit: attractive stress difference
Electricity is the orthogonal deformation mode of magnetism — together they constitute the complete linear response of shared link perturbations.
Definition 9.7: Strong Nuclear Force
When the distance between two topological knots shrinks to near-maximum shared link saturation:
•	Shared links form an indivisible shared network → disassembly requires simultaneous severing of large numbers of links → attraction
•	If distance shrinks further, shared link paths are forced to bend → repulsive bow stress → repulsion
The strong nuclear force is the nonlinear behavioral mode of shared links at extremely short ranges.
Definition 9.8: Weak Interaction
When the distance between two topological knots lies in the critical range where shared links are "just barely" established, the number of shared links is extremely small. This few-link state is metastable — any local reconnection perturbation may cause link breakage and trigger cascading disassembly.
The weak interaction is not a "force"; it is the spontaneous decay mode of metastable shared link networks.
Definition 9.9: Phenomenological Electric Current
In conductors, shared links on the surfaces of nuclear topological knots distribute along closed paths in the absence of external fields — the minimum-energy configuration of shared links in cylindrical geometry.
External fields — shared link density gradients — stretch these closed paths into helical paths along the conductor direction. Helically advancing shared links drag peripheral electron bricks into synchronous motion.
Electric current is the collective manifestation of shared link helical motion. Electrical resistance is the statistical frequency of collisions and reconnections during motion.
Definition 9.10: Superconductivity
When shared link helical motion achieves complete synchronization — all links have identical phase, collision frequency falls to zero — shared links enter a dissipationless transmission state.
Superconductivity is the synchronization phase transition of collective shared link motion modes.
