Part VII: Complete Lifecycle of the Membrane and Stellar Evolution Spectra
Axiom 10: Membrane Emergence Event
Within some region of the substrate, loop density due to intrinsic fluctuation — ensemble statistical fluctuations of local reconnection event rates — exceeds the critical value. Large numbers of loops of opposite directions form paired structures, compressed by external circulation to beyond their elastic recovery point. At the critical point, the system becomes unstable; the topological elastic potential drives the surface to turn outward and form a closed membrane.
This event is phenomenologically observed as the "origin" of the universe, but within the substrate of this framework there exists no temporal origin — membrane emergence is a phase transition of the substrate from one topological state to another. The phenomenological "origin" is the elastic release of the membrane from a hyper-knotted state; it is a phase transition, not a creation event.
Intuitive Dynamical Image: An unbounded vat of dense liquid substrate, driven by internal statistical fluctuations to a critical phase transition threshold, generates an expanding and ultimately dissipating topological bubble. This is the membrane emergence event of the universe.
Honest Annotation: "Intrinsic fluctuation" is the minimal description of initial conditions currently available. The model cannot explain why this particular region, this particular moment, a single membrane rather than multiple membranes. These are incompletenesses at the same level as the singularity problem in standard cosmology — however, TDMU possesses no singularity; only critical phase transitions.
Axiom 11: Evolution
The membrane expands continuously after formation. Expansion drives global reduction in membrane loop density — phenomenologically termed "cooling." Temperature is the local manifestation of loop density gradient.
During cooling, topological knots emerge sequentially: primordial bricks → electron bricks → leptons → mesons → baryons → atomic nuclei → atoms → molecules.
Each step constitutes the most stable weave structure "permitted to exist" at the current loop density.
The current membrane exists in the intermediate state where loop density is sufficiently low but has not yet reached membrane disassembly.
Axiom 12: The End
The membrane remains part of the substrate throughout. The circulation pressure from regions external to the membrane does not attenuate with membrane expansion. When membrane loop density falls below the critical threshold, the membrane's compressive strength drops below external circulation pressure, and the membrane enters irreversible structural instability.
The final outcome: global topological reconnection disassembly — the membrane fragments into discrete loops and returns to the substrate.
Supplement: The finality of global membrane disassembly refers only to the termination of the membrane as a coherent topological entity; it does not imply absolute prohibition of defect-complementary weaving between local topological knots. So long as two or more topological knots exist, the Path-I event rate R=NANBPmatchR=NANBPmatch remains mathematically greater than zero, though its characteristic timescale may far exceed the current membrane age. The global end is the termination of membrane structure, not absolute cessation of all topological interactions. The Path-I event rate RR in the woven solid remnant, while always positive, has its coherent time scale amplified exponentially by topological knot spacing far beyond the Hubble timescale.
Honest Annotation: The precise numerical values of the critical density, the dynamical path of instability, and the timescale of the end — none can be derived from the model. The model can only qualitatively predict the existence of an irreversible critical point.
7.1 Continuous Celestial Spectra: From Solid Planets to Quasars
Within TDMU, stellar evolution is refined as a dynamic game between Path I, Path II, fission, and Path III at different scales. All celestial bodies follow one fundamental phenomenological law: total inertia reduction converts to stress wave energy release. Path I, Path II, and their mixtures are simply different mechanisms for realizing this inertia-to-stress-wave conversion.
7.1.1 Solid Planets: Cutoff Conditions
Solid media possess two fatal weaknesses: extremely slow heat dissipation — heat conduction via lattice vibration, far less efficient than fluid convection — and severe thermal expansion constraint — lattice-restrained, preventing immediate density reduction from local temperature increase.
Once the Path-I reaction rate in a solid planetary core enters positive feedback — phenomenological temperature increase accelerates reaction, which further increases temperature — the solid medium can neither dissipate heat nor expand rapidly enough to reduce the reaction rate. Local stress rises exponentially, crossing the connecting-edge bulk fracture threshold in extremely brief intervals. Path II is triggered, and the entire planet disassembles layer by layer from the core outward.
Critical Condition: Solid planets possess an upper critical inertia limit. Beyond this inertia, regardless of initial phenomenological temperature, the core Path-I thermal runaway velocity exceeds lattice heat conduction velocity, triggering irreversible positive feedback until Path II initiates.
Survival Condition: If a solid planet's inertia lies below the critical value, or if internal radioactive decay/tidal heating suffices to melt the core, the medium acquires fluid characteristics — convective heat dissipation and thermal expansion response activate. Path I's negative feedback mechanism establishes, and the body may stably exist.
7.1.2 Gas Giants: Smoldering Topological Furnaces
Fluid media dissipate heat rapidly; upon heating and expansion, density decrease automatically down-regulates reaction rates. From mini-Neptunes to hot Jupiters to brown dwarfs, gas giants stably exist across broad inertia ranges.
Phenomenological observations: Jupiter's internal heat flow ∼5.5×1017∼5.5×1017 W; Saturn's internal heat flow 2.84±0.202.84±0.20 W/m². Path I provides a unified explanation: both Jupiter and Saturn cores host Path-I defect-complementary weaving reactions, differing only in macroscopic event rates.
7.1.3 The Topological Furnace Spectrum
Phenomenological Sun, Jupiter, and Saturn constitute a complete spectrum from "burning" to "smoldering." Planets are not "failed stars." They are topological furnaces where gravitational compression precisely maintains nuclear burning in a smoldering rather than extinguished state.
7.1.4 Stars: Main Sequence Balance and Jump-path Burning
When a gas aggregate's inertia exceeds a critical threshold, core defect matching probability crosses a critical value — defect-complementary matching probability among light topological knots rises sufficiently to sustain the entire aggregate's luminosity.
The essence of main sequence stars: under conditions of rapid heat dissipation and immediate thermal expansion feedback provided by fluid media, Path-I defect matching probability achieves dynamic equilibrium with environmental thermodynamic response.
Jump-path Nuclear Burning: The nuclear flow in stellar cores is not a one-way path. Jump pathways permit aggregation between any two nuclides. Light nuclide fusion dominates luminosity; medium and heavy nuclide fusion proceeds in parallel at lower rates.
7.2 Stellar Dynamic Cycles: Two Contests and Re-ignition Cycles
The fate of a star is determined by two contests:
First Contest: External compressive energy input rate vs Path-I reaction release rate
•	If Path-I release rate ≥≥ external compressive input rate → stable burning
•	If external compressive input rate > Path-I release rate → net accumulation of internal stress in nuclides
Second Contest: Accumulated external compressive energy vs nuclide total compressive binding energy EcompEcomp
•	Contraction raises density and phenomenological temperature, continuously increasing connecting-edge stress
•	When accumulated compressive energy exceeds EcompEcomp → Path II triggered
•	If Path-II released energy > gravitational binding energy → explosion — phenomenological supernova
•	If Path-II released energy < gravitational binding energy → silent collapse
Re-ignition: Path II forcibly disassembles accumulated heavy nuclei into lighter fragments; these light fragments re-enter the Path-I feedstock pool and undergo reactions with higher matching probability, re-releasing energy. The star bypasses the "fusion trap."
Core Principle: There is no true "terminal state" — only switching between Path I and Path II.
7.3 Mixed-State Stars: Symbiosis of Path I and Path II
Stars do not simply divide into "Path I dominant" or "Path II dominant"; a continuous spectrum exists:
Star Type	Path I Fraction	Path II Fraction	Phenomenological Manifestation	Typical Representative
Pure Path I	~100%	~0%	Stable light emission	Main sequence
Path I dominant	>90%	<10%	Stable burning, occasional flares	Red giant core
Mixed state	50%-90%	10%-50%	Periodic luminosity variations	Cepheids, Miras
Path II dominant	<50%	>50%	Violent outburst, structural reorganization	Certain supernova remnants
Pure Path II	~0%	~100%	Extreme luminosity, internal storm	Quasars (early)
7.4 Compact Topological Stars and Woven Solid State
When Path-II released energy is insufficient to completely disperse the star, the remnant is compressed by gravitation to an extreme degree. In this extreme compression state, topological knots are compressed to their limit; surface structure collapses — defect area fraction approaches 100%, and the entire body enters a "fully surface-reconnectable" state.
Definition of Compact Topological Star: A compact aggregate of topological knots compressed to defect fraction near 100%, with no further distinction of "stable nuclides." In the extreme compression limit, surface weaving has been completely destroyed; all nodes are in the connection-saturated state — every edge has reached its maximum coordination number and cannot accept new connections. This is connection-saturated rigidity — the topological correspondent of phenomenological "degeneracy pressure."
Woven Solid State: When internal vortex networks within the compact topological star have fully decohered, topological knots freeze into a rigid solid weave network under gravitational compression. Superfluidity is lost; Path I regains macroscopic prominence, but lacking the ability to expand and dissipate heat in a solid medium, negative feedback fails, and energy accumulates as stress in the shell.
7.5 Quasars: Confined Recirculating Reconnection Storms
Quasars are the brightest celestial bodies in the universe, with phenomenological luminosities reaching 10401040–10411041 W and variability timescales as short as days. In the very early universe, primordial topological knot aggregates had Path-II chain reconnection waves blocked by the convergence effect of massive peripheral shared links, preventing penetration of the aggregate boundary and forming sustained internal recirculating reconnection storms. Fission cycles and Path III contributions combine to produce extreme luminosities.
Quasars do not pass through a phenomenological main-sequence stellar progenitor stage. In the complete TDMU lifecycle picture, quasars correspond to the "fully loaded active" stage — the core is a fully formed compact topological star, with the sheath as a dense accretion layer supplied at high rates.
7.6 Complete Lifecycle of Compact Objects (Six Stages)
Stage	Phenomenological Picture	Sheath State	Core State	Optical Manifestation
I. Supersaturation Masquerade	Extreme stars (R136a1, etc.)	Extremely dense, supply rate far above critical	Superfluid vortex condensate	Normal stellar spectrum
II. Fully Loaded Active	Quasars	Dense accretion layer, ample supply	Same, higher activity	Bright ring, strong jets
III. Starved Bare Core	Dormant topological star (Sgr A*)	Accretion zone largely depleted	Maintains superfluidity, beginning "undernourishment"	Extremely faint, occasional flares
IV. Vortex Decoherence	Transition compact object	No sheath	Superfluid collapse, vortex unwinding releases frozen energy	Gravitational well disappears, begins emitting
V. Woven Solid State	Respiration-state compact topological star (pulsar) / hot compact remnant	None	Solid rigid network, stress accumulation-release cycle	Periodic pulses / residual thermal glow
VI. Woven Solid Remnant	Cooling solid core	None	Path II permanently ceased; only unobservable Path I remains	No emission at all
Detailed Mechanism of Stage V (Pulsars):
Post-supernova solid compact remnant. Path I within the interior continues releasing heat in the solid medium; energy accumulates locally in the core. When local stress crosses the EcompEcomp threshold, Path II triggers, releasing a shock wave — velocity approximately 0.3c0.3c to cc. Upon reaching the surface, the shock wave generates a "shattered armor" spallation ejection; observers receive signal only from the extremely narrow line-of-sight direction, forming a pulse.
•	Pulse period is the stress accumulation time to trigger threshold, not rotational period.
•	Femtosecond precision of period stability arises from the law of large numbers: the total particle pool of the entire star ∼1057∼1057; Path-I events per period far exceed 10301030; statistical fluctuations naturally fall below femtosecond levels.
•	Energy cycle self-sustainability: Path-I total energy release per period exceeds Path-II single-shock energy by 5 to 18 orders of magnitude.
•	Phenomenological magnetars are manifestations of larger-scale Path-II triggering.
•	Phenomenological glitches are intermediate-scale events between normal pulsars and magnetar outbursts.
•	The phenomenological death line is the gradual degradation process where Path-II trigger frequency falls below detection threshold.
