Part VIII: Unified Picture of Compact Objects — From Masquerade Stars to Woven Solid Remnants
8.1 Evolution Reversal Theorem
Theorem 8.3 (Evolution Reversal Theorem):
In compact objects with inertia above the stellar critical threshold, the compact superfluid core precedes the sheath. Phenomenological spectral types — OB, WR, red supergiants — are functions of sheath masking degree, not functions of core evolutionary age.
Corollary 8.3 (The Nature of Extreme Stars):
Phenomenologically massive stars — M>100M⊙M>100M⊙ — and phenomenologically large-radius stars — R>1500R⊙R>1500R⊙ — are not evolved stars progressing toward compact objects, but rather "sheath-masked" primordial superfluid vortex condensates. The visible stellar surface is the product of the vortex core driving peripheral topological sheath defect-complementary weaving heat release.
•	The core is a globally phase-locked superfluid vortex fluid; released energy is secluded as vortex writhe-twist energy through the three-step mechanism, generating no radiation pressure.
•	Triple geometric confinement — gravitational self-confinement, topological saturation barrier, vortex short-range cohesion — ensures the structure never disassembles.
•	Volume is independently determined by sheath Path-I negative feedback balance, decoupled from core inertia.
•	After sheath depletion, the masquerade dissolves and the core is exposed — phenomenologically manifesting as sudden luminosity drop and disappearance from the HR diagram without explosive ejection.
8.2 Complete Stellar Evolution Flow
The following is the complete evolutionary sequence from nebular material to final remnant within TDMU:
Nebula → Core → Path I ignition → Continued nebular accretion → Path II activation → Sustained nebular accretion → Accretion disk evolves from thin to dense → Accretion disk Path I/Path II mixing → Masquerade star (supersaturation accretion) → Sheath dissipation → Topological star (superfluid vortex condensate) → Vortex decoherence (mass loss, vortex unwinding) → Layer-by-layer solidification from outside inward (woven solid state) → Compact topological star (pulsar/magnetar) → Woven solid remnant (cooling solid core, Path II permanently ceased)
The underlying mechanisms for each stage are as follows:
Stage	Underlying Mechanism
Nebula → Core	Shared link convergence increases topological knot density
Path I ignition	Collision frequency crosses threshold; defect-complementary weaving produces observable energy release
Path II activation	Core stress accumulation exceeds EcompEcomp

Accretion disk evolution	External material supply rate determines accretion layer density and thickness
Masquerade star	Core enters superfluid vortex phase, energy secluded; sheath Path I determines observed luminosity
Sheath dissipation	Sheath material stripped layer by layer; core exposed
Topological star	Superfluid vortex condensate; phenomenologically corresponds to "black hole"
Vortex decoherence	Mass loss reduces vortex density below critical limit; phase locking collapses from outside inward
Layer-by-layer solidification	Outer layers decohere first, freezing into rigid weave network; solidification front advances inward
Compact topological star	Woven solid state; Path I accumulates stress; Path II triggers periodically (pulsar)
Woven solid remnant	Inertia dissipated to point where Path II cannot trigger; Path-I event rate unobservable
8.3 The Silent State of Topological Stars and the Dissipation-Solidification Sequence
A topological star is a topological knot aggregate whose weaving activity is nearly frozen at the macroscopic level. Path II is completely silent — internal stress remains below the EcompEcomp threshold of all nuclides; forced chain reconnection no longer triggers. Path I never mathematically reaches zero (Axiom 12 supplement), but its event rate R=NANBPmatchR=NANBPmatch can be suppressed by increased topological knot spacing and reduced defect matching probability to levels far below the observational window of the current cosmic age, appearing macroscopically as "silent." In this state, the convergence effect of the shared link network is the only observable macroscopic manifestation — phenomenologically identified as pure gravitation.
The topological star does not depend on singularities or event horizons. It is the core exposed after sheath depletion of a masquerade star, the terminal exposed state of the "star → topological star" evolution reversal chain. The core of a topological star is a globally phase-locked superfluid vortex condensate (DijΘ=0DijΘ=0, Fcore→1Fcore→1), with released energy secluded as vortex writhe-twist energy through the three-step mechanism.
The Dissipation-Solidification Sequence from Topological Star to Compact Topological Star:
1.	Mass-loss-driven decoherence: Path-I events, though weak, occur continuously; each event consumes an extremely small portion of frozen vortex energy, manifesting as slow mass loss. When cumulative mass loss reduces vortex density below the critical limit nvcritnvcrit, global phase locking can no longer be maintained.
2.	Layer-by-layer solidification from outside inward: Phase locking collapse is not an instantaneous global event, but a phase transition that advances layer by layer from outside inward. Outer vortices unwind first; topological knots lose superfluid support and freeze into a rigid weave network under gravitational compression; this solidification front then advances inward, forming a radial layered structure of "solid shell, superfluid core."
3.	Completion of the woven solid state: When the solidification front reaches the core, the entire topological star transforms completely into the woven solid state — a solid rigid network in which Path-I accumulated stress periodically triggers Path II, manifesting macroscopically as a compact topological star (pulsar).
4.	Woven solid remnant: When inertia has further dissipated to the point where it can no longer sustain the stress accumulation required for Path II triggering, the compact topological star enters its final stage — Path II permanently ceases; Path-I event rate R>0R>0 remains mathematically true but is completely unobservable. The body becomes a cooling solid woven core, macroscopically emitting no light.
Therefore, the topological star and the compact topological star are not different names for the same physical state, but two successive phases of the same entity in the dissipation-solidification sequence:
•	Topological star: Superfluid vortex phase; energy secluded; macroscopically silent but gravitationally manifest. Phenomenologically corresponds to "black hole."
•	Compact topological star: Woven solid phase; superfluidity lost; Path I accumulates macroscopically; Path II triggers periodically. Phenomenologically corresponds to "neutron star/pulsar."
Critical Adjudication: The standard-model chain "supernova explosion → neutron star" is revised in TDMU to "supernova explosion (sheath slippage event) exposes topological star core → topological star undergoes dissipation-solidification sequence → compact topological star (pulsar)." The supernova event itself does not produce the compact remnant; the compact remnant is the final product of the subsequent dissipation-solidification process.
8.4 Unified Criterion for Two-Layer Masquerade States
Stellar-Scale Two-Layer Masquerade — phenomenological extreme stars: R136a1, Stephenson 2-18:
•	Core: topological star (superfluid vortex condensate, energy secluded)
•	Sheath: supersaturated accreting material; surface Path-I negative feedback self-stabilizing
•	Luminosity and radius determined solely by sheath, decoupled from core inertia
Galactic-Scale Two-Layer Masquerade — phenomenological red monsters: JWST extreme reddened galaxies in the early universe:
•	Core: rapidly growing supermassive topological star (energy secluded)
•	Sheath: massive gas and dust continuously burning under Path-I negative feedback self-stabilization
•	Jump pathways fully open in the early universe; galactic cores aggregating at exponential rates
Adjudication Criterion — Quasars vs Red Monsters:
•	Quasars: Core in "superfluid vortex engine fully loaded operation"; Path-II storm confined by gravitational potential well. Luminosity derives from release and reabsorption of the confined storm itself (internal circulation).
•	Red Monsters: Core in "superfluid vortex engine nascent growth" or "starved accumulation" state; internal storm has not yet triggered at large scale. Extreme luminosity and infrared radiation derive entirely from external sheath — Path-I negative feedback combustion — masking and thermalization.
8.5 The Silent State of Topological Stars
Topological knot aggregates with weaving activity nearly frozen. Path II completely silent; Path-I event rate suppressed to unobservability. Only the convergence effect of the shared link network remains, manifesting as phenomenological pure gravitation. No singularity, no event horizon. This is the terminal state after sheath depletion of masquerade stars, or after compact topological star inertia exceeds the disruption window upper limit.
Supermassive topological stars are galactic-scale correspondents — nodes of galactic-scale shared link networks where massive topological knot aggregates converge to connection saturation.
8.6 Active Galactic Nuclei: Transition States
Galactic nuclei with phenomenological activity between quasars and silent galaxies. Core is a topological star; sheath material continuously falls through the fission surface, triggering local Path II near the fission surface and generating intermittent high-energy radiation and jets. The object is in the transition phase from "fully loaded active" to "starved bare core."
8.7 MACHOs: Unified Classification of Compact Objects
MACHOs — Massive Compact Halo Objects — encompass three classes of objects, all normal evolutionary products of TDMU:
MACHO Phenomenological Class	TDMU Correspondence	Mechanism
Brown dwarf MACHO	Smoldering state	Path I smoldering but inertia insufficient to reach main-sequence threshold
Compact remnant MACHO	Respiration-state or already degraded compact topological star	Woven solid state; intermittent or ceased Path-II triggering
Black-hole-mass MACHO	Topological star (superfluid vortex condensate)	Identical in essence to topological stars formed through stellar evolution. Early-universe jump pathways permit direct compression formation, constituting no separate physical category
These objects are naturally distributed throughout galactic halos as components of non-luminous compact objects; their number density and spatial distribution are determined by the statistical weights of early-universe jump pathways.
