Part IX: Testable Predictions
The following testable predictions are presented under the TDMU framework. Each is formulated as "Prediction" or "If...then..." to specify clear falsifiability conditions.
9.1 Gravitation and Membrane Expansion
Prediction 9.1 (Spatial Variation of the Gravitational Constant): The effective gravitational constant in dense environments should exceed the solar-system value.
Prediction 9.2 (Local-Global Hubble Constant Discrepancy): Local measurements should systematically exceed CMB-inferred values, with the difference proportional to local loop density fluctuations.
Prediction 9.3 (Non-constant Dark Energy Equation of State): w(z)≠−1w(z)=−1 with time evolution.
Prediction 9.4 (Inflexion in Membrane Expansion Deceleration Parameter): q(z)q(z) should exhibit an inflexion from negative to positive at some redshift.
9.2 Extreme Stars and Evolution Reversal
Prediction 9.5 (Seismic Inertia vs Luminosity Inertia): For extreme stars, seismic inertia MseismoMseismo should significantly exceed luminosity inertia MlumMlum, with ratio Mseismo/Mlum>1.5Mseismo/Mlum>1.5, increasing monotonically with radius. If the ratio approximates unity, the hidden-core hypothesis fails.
Prediction 9.6 (Absence of Standard Neutrino Signature in Outburst Events): Large-radius stellar outbursts should not involve proton-electron capture processes in the core. Detection of a neutrino signal consistent with collapse models would falsify the framework.
Prediction 9.7 (Terminal Optical Obscuration): After sheath depletion, the object's luminosity should drop sharply on thermal timescales, disappearing directly from the HR diagram without explosive ejection.
Prediction 9.8 (Anomalously Weak Radio Emission from Extreme Stars): No collimated jet structure should be present — due to extremely thick sheath absorption and thermalization.
9.3 Compact Objects and Pulsars
Prediction 9.9 (Finite Photon Shell Thickness): The photon capture shell should possess finite thickness ΔRph>0ΔRph>0, diverging from the zero-thickness photon sphere of general relativity. EHT and next-generation space VLBI could measure this.
Prediction 9.10 (Compact Object Inertia Gap): In the phenomenological ∼2.5–5M⊙∼2.5–5M⊙ interval, stable compact objects should be scarce; any discovered objects should exhibit transition-state characteristics. Discovery of abundant stable compact objects in this interval would require framework revision.
Prediction 9.11 (Pulse Width Independence from Period): Short-period and long-period pulsars should exhibit similar pulse-width distributions.
Prediction 9.12 (Glitch Precursors): Prior to large-scale glitches, a systematic increase in pulse intensity should be observed — a signal of accumulating core stress.
9.4 Quasars and Active Galactic Nuclei
Prediction 9.13 (Non-Accretion-Disk Nature of Quasars): If quasar spectra and variability are rigorously proven consistent with the accretion disk model, and EHT imaging confirms event horizon characteristics at quasar centers, the framework would require revision.
Prediction 9.14 (Quasar Light Curve Chaoticity): Light curves should exhibit red-noise spectra or chaotic behavior, not single periodic peaks.
9.5 Planets and Stars
Prediction 9.15 (Solid Planet Inertia Upper Limit): Pure solid planets above a critical inertia should be unable to stably exist. Discovery of large populations of long-term stable pure solid planets at any inertia interval without cutoff characteristics would falsify the framework.
Prediction 9.16 (Saturn Seasonal Variation Recurrence): Saturn's internal heat flow seasonal variation of 16.0±4.2%16.0±4.2% should recur stably over its 29.5-year orbital period.
9.6 Quantum and Superconductivity
Prediction 9.17 (Exchange Parity and Pauli Exclusion): In any stable atom, electron bricks must occupy distinct spatial winding modes; shell filling numbers should strictly follow 2n22n2. Discovery of a stable configuration with two electron bricks occupying identical spatial winding modes and zero phase difference in any stable atom — i.e., systematic violation of Pauli exclusion — would falsify the framework.
Prediction 9.18 (Flux Quantization Structure): Magnetic flux must be quantized as integer multiples of an irreducible fundamental unit.
9.7 Membrane Cosmology Probes
Prediction 9.19 (Primordial Transverse Wave Circular Polarization): Primordial shared-link transverse waves or CMB should exhibit weak circular polarization components.
Prediction 9.20 (Compact Remnant Number Density): Galactic halos should contain large populations of low-inertia (substellar-scale) isolated compact objects — including compact topological stars and woven solid remnants — whose total mass contribution suffices to explain the observed galactic rotation curve characteristics. Their inertia spectral function is determined by the statistical weights of early-universe jump pathways.
